DXVK 601930949d111edbbcf9dd463948426d9f8f6ddd

A DXGI factory creates the Vulkan instance in its constructor, and every
adapter it lists holds a Vulkan adapter. A process that only lists adapters
and outputs, as a browser's main process does for its displays, then loads
the Vulkan driver: on a PS5 the GPU client, an image of about 23 MiB that is
wired, so about 20 MiB of each such helper's flexible-memory census. In
a launcher's start on the PS5 Pro (2026-10-20), 18 of its processes loaded
DXGI and the driver ("Found device" logged, no "Creating device"), and the
census ended its browser 17 times at 868-902 MiB of a 870/940 MiB limit.

DXVK_DXGI_ADAPTER_SUMMARY names a file that holds what DXGI needs of each
adapter before a device exists: its DxvkAdapterInfo, whether it is linked to
a discrete GPU, and its linked integrated GPU's LUID. A factory that finds
the file lists adapters, matches monitors and describes adapters and outputs
from it, and creates the instance, and an adapter its Vulkan adapter, only
when something needs Vulkan: a device (GetDXVKAdapter), a swap chain, the
Vulkan interop, the memory budget. A factory that creates the instance
writes the file (a temporary file, then a replacing move). Without the
variable, or with a file of another size or version, nothing changes. The
file's name must change with the driver and DXVK; PS5_Proton puts it in the
runtime's cache namespace (src/pw_shader_cache.c).

The factory's monitors and outputs go through the WSI driver, which only the
instance started (wsi::init); a factory without an instance dereferenced a
null driver in wsi::enumMonitors (the standard PS5, 2026-10-20: the
browser's main process, a null read at dxgi+0x67fc9 from DxgiFactory's
constructor). Each factory now holds its own WSI reference, and the WSI
reference count, which a factory and the instance can now take on different
threads, is taken under a lock.

